WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:13.420
- Hi, everyone, Sal Khan here
for our daily livestream.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:16.020
And just as a reminder of what this is

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.230
for some of you all who might be new is

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:23.030
as the school closures have
kind of rolled out around

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:24.470
not just the country but the world,

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.110
we realized that there is a lot of

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:30.280
demand for folks to also feel a little bit

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.770
more connected to each other.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.870
And there's just a lot of
information going around

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:34.930
around the school closures,

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:36.730
there's a lot of materials
that we're putting out,

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.660
and it would be nice to have a place

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.150
where we can all connect,
at least once a day,

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:44.060
to make sure that we can
answer each other's questions,

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:45.330
and get some of those announcements out.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:46.430
And so we're calling this something

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.190
of a national or a global homeroom.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.900
And the whole point is
to ask your questions.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.860
I have fellow team members who are looking

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.000
at the questions you're asking on Facebook

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.680
and in YouTube and on Twitter,
and we will get to them.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.360
We're also going to be
bringing in some guests.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.360
Now, one thing I do like to remind folks

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.980
is Khan Academy is a not-for-profit.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.140
We are funded by philanthropic donations.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:10.780
It costs a lot more money
than you might think

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.840
to create a platform that can serve

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.530
hundreds of millions of people.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:16.730
Khan Academy is much more than me.

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:18.490
We're over 200 full-time employees,

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:19.970
thousands of volunteers.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:22.710
Just our server costs are
many, many millions of dollars

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.080
and it looks like our traffic,

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.420
because of school closures is two X

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.810
and approaching three X of
what it normally would be.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:31.140
So we definitely need your help.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.360
I want to do a quick thank you

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.000
to some corporate partners
who have stepped up

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.010
very quickly to help us
fund some of that gap,

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:38.130
although we need more help

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.820
from donations of all sizes.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.480
But special thanks to Bank of America,

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.450
AT&amp;T, google.org, and Novartis,

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.130
who have been some of
the first to step up.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:51.430
But we need more help
from you and from others.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:56.420
Now, the focus of today's livestream

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.540
is actually how do we
help teachers and parents

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:02.510
get set up so that they
could help their students.

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:03.560
We've been talking about that

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.030
pretty much in every livestream so far.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:08.140
I've talked about these
webinars that we've run

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.740
in the previous few weeks.

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:12.120
We've talked about some of
the resources we've put out

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.370
like the daily schedule.

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:14.380
All of this is available

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.340
if you just go to khanacademy.org.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.050
You'll see on our homepage.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:19.350
You'll also see a little a banner,

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.220
regardless of where you
are at khanacademy.org,

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:23.370
that will point to some
of these resources.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:32.660
Teeter professional learning
at Khan Academy, Meaghan.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:34.260
And so Meaghan will join us.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:35.850
This will be a great chance,

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.270
and everyone can ask questions,

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:39.300
students can ask questions,
parents and teachers,

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.270
but especially we're gonna start

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:41.510
for some of the questions from parents

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:43.620
and teachers on how do you get set up,

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.700
how do you look at student dashboards,

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:48.630
what are best practices, things like that.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.200
And as I wait for Meaghan to get on,

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.490
I will give a few more announcements.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:56.560
Above and beyond this
livestream at Khan Academy,

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.860
we're trying to see what other
supports can we give you.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.730
So yesterday was the
first time that we ran

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:05.060
a virtual classroom for algebra students.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.250
These are students in
Algebra I or Algebra II,

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:08.870
and this was at 10 a.m. Pacific.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:10.960
We did it yesterday, and we were able

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:12.940
to cover some examples, answer questions

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:14.990
about systems of equations.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:17.119
Tomorrow we're going to be doing again,

00:03:17.119 --> 00:03:19.390
you can see an example
of what it looks like.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.750
We actually, it really
did feel like a classroom,

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.510
and I think there was
about a thousand students.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:26.550
So we're all experimenting
with new things,

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:29.260
and I was able to answer a lot
of the student's questions.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.670
Tomorrow we're gonna do it again.

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:32.860
We got a lotta positive feedback from it.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.650
So on Thursday, at once
again 10:00 a.m. Pacific,

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.370
and tomorrow's topic is actually going

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:40.920
to be factoring and graphing quadratics.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.450
It's not just me giving a lecture.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:44.650
We're gonna give you a problem to work on,

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:46.770
for students to work on,
they're gonna be able

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.990
to fill out surveys on what
they think the answer is,

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:51.840
they're gonna be able to vote on questions

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.670
for me to answer, so we're
gonna try to make it feel

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:59.270
as much like a live, interactive
classroom as possible.

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:00.620
With that, I'd love to bring on Meaghan,

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:03.060
and we can start answering some questions.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.056
Meaghan, are you there?

00:04:04.056 --> 00:04:05.306
(laughs) Oh hey, Meaghan.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.150
- Hi Sal, and hi everyone.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:09.710
How are you?

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:10.543
- Good, good, good.

00:04:10.543 --> 00:04:11.720
And maybe a good place to start,

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:14.130
I just talked about the Algebra I

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:15.797
and Algebra II virtual classrooms,

00:04:15.797 --> 00:04:18.900
but we're also leading webinars
for teachers and parents.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.710
If you could tell us a
little bit about those?

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:23.110
- Absolutely, Sal.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:26.140
We know that this is a pretty chaotic time

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.890
for everyone, students, parents, teachers,

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:31.360
and we are trying to offer opportunities

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.680
for parents and teachers,
as well as students,

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:37.300
to ask their questions and find a space

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.270
where they can share some of the things

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:40.660
that are going through
their heads right now

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.250
and how we might be able
to best support them

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:44.720
through this transition.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.020
And so over the past couple weeks,

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:50.810
we've had a couple of webinars
around getting started

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.660
on Khan Academy as both
a parent and a teacher,

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:57.640
and then we ran one last Friday on tips

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:01.240
for effective remote
teaching and learning.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:03.640
The recordings for all
of those are available

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:07.432
on the site, along with
some supporting resources.

00:05:07.432 --> 00:05:09.160
Coming up in the next couple of days,

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:12.510
this afternoon, we have
office hours, again,

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:15.410
for teachers to get
their questions answered

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:18.310
by some of our ambassadors,
our really exceptional,

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:19.143
you know,

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:27.820
and then we have another webinar tomorrow

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:30.090
from one of our ambassadors talking about

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:32.820
some of the best ways for teachers

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.590
to get effective results
using Khan Academy.

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:36.500
And keep an eye out.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:40.640
We'll be looking to add another
parent webinar next week

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:43.600
to help parents get started
with both Khan Academy

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.373
and Khan Academy Kids.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:48.280
- Awesome. Thanks, Meaghan.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:50.670
And maybe just a starting question

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:52.830
that we've gotten many versions

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:55.380
of what I'm about to ask you.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:57.110
But a lot of teachers and parents

00:05:57.110 --> 00:05:59.540
are just feeling a little overwhelmed.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.100
Schools closed with not a lotta notice,

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:02.560
not a lotta planning,
this is a new frontier

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:03.603
for all of us.

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:07.830
For speaking to a teacher, and
to some degree a parent now

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:09.380
who is feeling overwhelmed,

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:11.550
how should they even think
about remote learning,

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.400
and what steps might they wanna take?

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.620
- I think that's a really key point, Sal,

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.377
is that a lot of people
are feeling overwhelmed,

00:06:18.377 --> 00:06:20.510
and there wasn't a lotta
time for preparation.

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:23.100
And teachers, in
particulars, are preparers.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:24.820
As a classroom teacher for nine years,

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:27.410
I couldn't imagine the
shock of transitioning

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:29.900
from being in a classroom
brick and mortar space

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:33.130
with my students to jumping
fully remote so quickly.

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:35.917
I think one thing that I've
heard from a lot of teachers

00:06:35.917 --> 00:06:38.293
and a lot of parents
is take a deep breath.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.290
It's not the expectation
that you are going

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:42.860
to cover everything you would cover

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:44.580
in your classroom right now.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.640
It's really important to cut yourself

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.410
and your students some slack,

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:49.800
and just know that the fact

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:51.490
that you are taking the endeavor

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:53.300
of getting them actively learning

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:57.280
and focusing on some key
concepts is really great,

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:00.070
and that you're taking time to embrace

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:02.490
kind of the emotional impact for both you

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:04.750
as a parent or a teacher and your students

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:08.290
and building in some time
into your time with students

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:10.480
to be able to reflect with them,

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:12.620
and you know, take some time as you shared

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:13.780
in some of the schedules,

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.310
take some time to reflect on
what's going on right now,

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:19.113
and incorporate that into
the learning experience.

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:22.300
I'd say the other piece
that I would really, really

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.600
focus on is communication.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.180
I fear sometimes that working remotely

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.570
with students and teachers
gives the illusion

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:32.040
that there's less to need to communicate.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:35.670
But in fact, we really need
more communication right now

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:38.130
and taking the time to either connect live

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:41.340
with students or send
a message to students,

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:43.120
you know, finding opportunities

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:45.770
to create that human
connection even if you're not

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:49.063
in the same physical space
is so important right now.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:53.650
- I'm getting a question
here from Debbie Charlton

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:55.670
who's actually one of the Khan Academy

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:57.609
teacher ambassadors, and Debbie's asking--

00:07:57.609 --> 00:07:59.540
- Yeah!

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:03.917
- You know Debbie, "How
can you assign content

00:08:03.917 --> 00:08:06.827
"to specific students based
on what they have not worked

00:08:06.827 --> 00:08:09.020
"on yet but others may have?"

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:12.250
So we have this situation
where, I guess always

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:13.830
kids might be at different levels,

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:14.877
might be working at different things,

00:08:14.877 --> 00:08:16.260
and to some degree we support that,

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:17.890
but how does a teacher maybe assign that,

00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:19.090
or should they assign that?

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:20.490
How do you think about that?

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:22.730
- Yeah, I think it's a good question

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:25.760
that we think about not just in this time

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:30.230
but especially now where you
have the additional challenge

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:32.900
of not being able to see that student.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:35.470
I think what is really
great about Khan Academy

00:08:35.470 --> 00:08:37.943
is the ability to assign
these broad mastery goals.

00:08:37.943 --> 00:08:40.200
When we think about what
are the students trying

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.300
to accomplish for the year.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:44.830
Are they trying to, you
know, work on Algebra I?

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:46.660
Are they trying to
complete third grade math

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:48.050
or maybe high school biology?

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:50.040
Our mastery extends beyond math.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:53.240
And so I think starting
with that mastery goal,

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:56.160
which allows students to
work at their own time

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:00.360
and pace, and helps them
identify areas of opportunity.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:02.810
And we have some resources
right on the site,

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:05.640
right from that banner when
you get to Khan Academy,

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:09.320
that will connect you and
how to set up a mastery goal.

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:11.090
So any teacher could leverage that

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:12.790
as a really good starting point,

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:16.310
and then once you're able
to see students progress,

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:19.310
and we see maybe after
three to five sessions

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:22.000
on working towards that master goal,

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.870
the teacher reporting will allow teachers

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:27.930
to identify growth opportunities,

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:31.980
so skills that students might
need additional support with,

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:33.920
and from there, there's an assign button,

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:36.980
and teachers can import
the individual students,

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:40.230
small groups of students,
or the entire class

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:43.520
with aligned exercise to identify

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:46.550
and support learning in that gap area.

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:49.340
So the short version is
assign a mastery goal,

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:52.210
and use the data to make
supporting assignments.

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:54.990
And we have, again, we
have some resources right

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:57.600
on our website that walk
teachers through this,

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.840
and some specifically to support

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:01.943
in that remote learning situation.

00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:05.610
- And we have another
kind of related question,

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:08.050
also from Debbie, which is a
question I think many teachers

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:11.550
are asking which is how does
it work with Google Classroom?

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:12.660
We just talked about assignments

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:13.493
and to what degree should you

00:10:13.493 --> 00:10:15.020
when kids are at different levels,

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:18.330
but what support or integration
do we or don't we have

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:19.360
with Google Classroom?

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:20.560
Or how would you use it?

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:23.210
- It's a good question.

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:25.400
We do integrate with Google Classroom,

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:28.030
and the best thing about that is that

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:30.790
we know a lot of teachers are establishing

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:32.700
their classrooms on Khan Academy now,

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:34.970
and (chuckles) as a
teacher, I can empathize

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:36.770
with the setup and fostering and getting

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:38.700
to norms with students.

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:40.320
If your students are already comfortable

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:43.270
with Google Classroom, you
can connect Google Classroom

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:44.650
directly to Khan Academy.

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:46.020
You don't have to add a new roster,

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:47.680
you don't have to create a new class,

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:50.420
as you set up your class there's an option

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:52.130
that says Connect with Google Classroom,

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:55.530
and it will import your roster directly.

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:58.030
In a time where students
are already having

00:10:58.030 --> 00:10:59.980
to learn so many new tools,

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:01.650
and we want them to focus, right,

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:05.190
more on learning the content
than learning new technology.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:07.370
By integrating Khan Academy

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:09.170
with your Google Classroom account,

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:11.510
students can access that in the same way

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:12.700
they've already been using it.

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:15.330
So you're taking a step
of stress of off you

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:17.073
and off of your students.

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:19.113
- Awesome.

00:11:19.113 --> 00:11:21.470
I know we're getting a
lotta questions here.

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:24.017
One of 'em, this is from Sono Gatelli.

00:11:24.017 --> 00:11:25.927
"As a parent, where to
start on Khan Academy

00:11:25.927 --> 00:11:27.487
"as a roadmap for seventh a grader?

00:11:27.487 --> 00:11:30.060
"Are their live classes Khan
Academy's planning to offer?"

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:32.313
I could maybe take a
first stab at that one.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:35.660
As many of y'all know,
we've published schedules

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:37.450
for students of different age categories,

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:41.260
and so we do have one that a
seventh grader would fall in.

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:42.980
I think one of the things
we are thinking about

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:45.320
is can we start, especially
now that it looks like

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:47.720
some of the school
closures might be longer

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:49.419
and maybe go through the summer,

00:11:49.419 --> 00:11:51.440
we are thinking about
maybe we could create

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:54.170
some goals or learning plans for students,

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:55.640
but those haven't been released yet.

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:57.650
We're trying to do everything we can

00:11:57.650 --> 00:11:59.480
with the resources we have.

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:03.270
On terms of live classes, these
livestreams, these webinars,

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:07.670
these classrooms we're doing,
we started with algebra.

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:08.970
This is really just an experiment.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:10.130
We started the first one yesterday.

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:11.470
We're gonna do another one tomorrow,

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:13.220
and we're going to see where this goes,

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:16.480
what capabilities we have on
our side to do more and more,

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:19.837
but I don't wanna jump the gun on it

00:12:19.837 --> 00:12:22.710
'cause we are very resource
constrained right now.

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:24.390
But I would love to figure out ways

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:26.950
to support middle school students as well.

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:29.860
Algebra we picked because
that's a major area

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:31.750
that's foundational for a lotta folks,

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:34.230
but obviously, so is middle
school math, pre-algebra,

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:35.650
and obviously, not just math.

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:39.020
There's other subjects as well
that might be interesting.

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:39.930
Let's see.

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:42.260
We have a question

00:12:43.750 --> 00:12:44.603
from,

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:48.300
from Facebook.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.280
So it says, actually it just got modified,

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:52.527
from Laura Hall.

00:12:52.527 --> 00:12:54.517
"Our school wants us to filter everything

00:12:54.517 --> 00:12:55.937
"through Google Classroom so it's more

00:12:55.937 --> 00:12:57.627
"of a one-stop shop.

00:12:57.627 --> 00:12:59.127
"I'd love to be able to link
their assignments straight

00:12:59.127 --> 00:13:00.067
"to Google from Khan.

00:13:00.067 --> 00:13:02.200
"Any chance that could become available?"

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:04.150
Well, that's essentially very similar

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:05.040
to what you had just answered.

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:06.490
And the simple answer is yes.

00:13:08.212 --> 00:13:10.790
- While we're able to link
their rosters currently,

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:13.000
the assignments don't show up directly

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:15.820
in Google Classroom, when
we know that's a common ask

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:16.653
for teachers.

00:13:16.653 --> 00:13:17.960
We've heard that even prior

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:20.173
to this remote learning transition,

00:13:21.534 --> 00:13:23.660
and I know that there's
a lot going on right now,

00:13:23.660 --> 00:13:26.110
and that would be a really
helpful piece for teachers.

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:29.550
We do find that the connecting
with Google Classroom

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:31.410
allows the roster to be, you know, brought

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:33.190
in really easily, and
that students can login

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:36.120
to Khan Academy simply by
using Google's single sign-on,

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:38.760
and they'll see their assignments
the moment they log in

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:40.540
right on their learner home page.

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:41.820
So that way students don't have

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:43.540
to go digging around for that.

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:45.480
So while it's not a direct connection

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:48.050
to posting the assignments
in Google Classroom,

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:50.993
it does really become
low-lift for the students.

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:54.000
- Great. Thanks, Meaghan.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:54.950
So let's see.

00:13:54.950 --> 00:13:56.260
There's a couple of questions here.

00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:57.680
These go a little bit,

00:13:57.680 --> 00:13:59.300
these are more student-facing questions,

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:00.960
but we could try to answer a couple

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:02.380
of these really quick.

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:04.687
Let's see. From YouTube,
Caras Alexander says,

00:14:04.687 --> 00:14:07.150
"How is Khan Academy
helping out with AP exams?"

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:09.650
Well Caras, we've had content for many

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:12.310
of the core AP exams even
before this crisis hit,

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:16.640
AP calculus, AP stats, AP
biology, chemistry, physics,

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:19.090
micro, macro economics, CS principles,

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:21.010
American history, civics, and government.

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:22.140
I think I got all of them.

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:24.410
I might be, oh and art history.

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:25.810
I think I got all of 'em.

00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:27.570
So we've always had that content

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:29.470
that you can learn at
your own time and pace,

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:31.760
use the unit test, use
the course challenges,

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:33.020
the mastery challenges to make sure

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:36.020
that you've really digested
a lot of that material,

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:38.410
and even before this
crisis hit, historically,

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:40.580
as we've gone into AP exams we have tried

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:42.850
to run some webinars and livestreams

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:44.840
for students at right when
they're trying to prepare.

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:46.880
And so we're going to try to explore

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:50.170
that type of thing for this year as well.

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:51.003
Let's see.

00:14:51.003 --> 00:14:52.360
Other questions on YouTube.

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:54.807
Courageous Qbert says, "Is it okay for me

00:14:54.807 --> 00:14:57.270
"to study physics in seventh grade?"

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:58.880
My two cents, yeah!

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:02.040
If you're ready to study
physics in seventh grade,

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:03.000
no one should stop you.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:03.973
That's great!

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:07.450
You know, what I would say is
to do physics on Khan Academy.

00:15:07.450 --> 00:15:08.790
We have the algebra based physics.

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:11.740
You should definitely know
the basics of algebra,

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:15.370
know the basics of quadratics,

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:17.510
things like parabolas,
factoring quadratics,

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:19.440
and know the basics of trigonometry,

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:21.010
things like sine, cosine, tangent.

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:23.730
If you know those things
you actually are ready

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:26.670
for physics, high school level physics.

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:29.060
If you don't know those
things, you actually could jump

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:31.630
in and learn those units,
those particular units,

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:34.880
and actually you'll
have the math background

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:37.370
to engage on the physics side.

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:40.640
Let's see. Other questions for

00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:43.270
we have,

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:45.037
well, Astro Jaden on YouTube

00:15:45.037 --> 00:15:46.457
is asking a pretty big question.

00:15:46.457 --> 00:15:47.747
"What was the origin of Khan Academy?

00:15:47.747 --> 00:15:48.717
"How did you come up with the idea

00:15:48.717 --> 00:15:51.490
"of creating a nonprofit organization?"

00:15:51.490 --> 00:15:53.820
I'll answer it as concisely as I can

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:56.718
'cause I've been known to
talk about this for (laughs)

00:15:56.718 --> 00:15:59.570
a very long time.

00:15:59.570 --> 00:16:01.490
Many of y'all know it started as a project

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:03.710
with my family members.

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:05.947
I kept thinking, "What
more tools could I make

00:16:05.947 --> 00:16:07.020
"for my family members?"

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:10.280
It's eerily similar to
the situation we're in now

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:11.997
where we're just like,
"Okay, there's a big need.

00:16:11.997 --> 00:16:13.407
"What tools can we create,

00:16:13.407 --> 00:16:15.197
"and then how can we
make it serve the needs

00:16:15.197 --> 00:16:16.030
"of more and more people?"

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:17.900
But this was back with me and one cousin

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:19.550
and then more, word got around my family

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:20.757
free tutoring was going on,

00:16:20.757 --> 00:16:22.850
and I found myself with many folks.

00:16:22.850 --> 00:16:25.030
And so I started writing the exercises

00:16:25.030 --> 00:16:26.700
on Khan Academy, an early version.

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:28.680
That was what Khan Academy
was initially about,

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:31.480
and it was a friend that
recommended I make videos

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:33.130
to supplement those exercises,

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:35.660
and I thought that was just a silly idea.

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:39.782
As I often say, I said, "YouTube
is for cats playing piano."

00:16:39.782 --> 00:16:41.630
(laughs) But I got over the
idea that it wasn't my idea,

00:16:41.630 --> 00:16:43.700
gave it a shot, and that
took on a life of its own.

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:46.340
And then by 2009, 2010 there were hundreds

00:16:46.340 --> 00:16:47.940
of thousands of people using it.

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:50.070
I had trouble focusing on my day job,

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:51.800
which was really being, I was an analyst

00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:54.810
at an investment firm, and so I sat down

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:56.327
with my wife who said,
"Hey, maybe we should start

00:16:56.327 --> 00:16:58.627
"this off as a nonprofit.

00:16:58.627 --> 00:17:01.467
"Hopefully I'll find
people who can donate."

00:17:01.467 --> 00:17:03.060
And I took the plunge.

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:04.930
The whole reason I wanted
to it as a not-for-profit,

00:17:04.930 --> 00:17:06.510
I do live in Silicone Valley,

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:10.560
people were offering to fund
it as a for profit business,

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:13.400
and it just, you know, for this particular

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:16.930
role in society it felt
like the wrong thing to do.

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:19.050
There's nothing wrong for
for profit businesses,

00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:20.960
but for something in
education where you don't

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:24.470
wanna make it based on who
has the ability to pay,

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:26.190
you wanna help level the playing field,

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:27.610
you wanna have universal access.

00:17:27.610 --> 00:17:28.580
If someone wants to learn,

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:30.100
there should be no frictions to it.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:31.780
That was the point.

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:33.860
We set up this mission,
free world class education

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:35.040
for anyone, anywhere,

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:37.157
and it was somewhat delusional (laughs)

00:17:37.157 --> 00:17:39.090
10, 11 years ago for a guy operating

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:40.600
out of a walk-in closet,

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:42.970
but over the last 10 years with the help

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:45.680
of this really incredible
team at Khan Academy,

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:47.814
many, many thousands of
people who've donated

00:17:47.814 --> 00:17:50.340
to this effort, and we need more help,

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:52.860
we've been able to make I think a bit

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:55.410
of a dent and really
scale to a lot of folks.

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:58.930
You know, I think this
whole school closure crisis

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:02.020
due to COVID-19 highlights,
I think, the importance

00:18:02.020 --> 00:18:05.580
of us existing (laughs),
the work that this team

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:09.450
has been able to do,
and why it was important

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:10.420
that we're not-for-profit.

00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:11.480
Because there's a lot going on at EdTech

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:14.110
and a lotta people doing
really great things,

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:16.850
but in this time of school
closures you need something

00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:18.640
that is universally accessible,

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:21.340
and part of that is it has to be free,

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:23.260
and that's where we're fairly unique,

00:18:23.260 --> 00:18:24.410
and you know, truly free.

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:27.720
So no shady stuff with
ads, no kind of free

00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:30.340
but then there might be some
payment for better things,

00:18:30.340 --> 00:18:33.340
no "we'll sell your data
type" of free, none of that,

00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:36.620
truly free and not doing
anything shady with data.

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:37.920
That's important right now.

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:40.770
You have to have a breadth
and depth of subjects.

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:43.710
We have gone into many
subjects and in many areas

00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:45.550
where there might not be a business model,

00:18:45.550 --> 00:18:48.550
so other EdTech players
haven't gone there.

00:18:48.550 --> 00:18:49.890
And so that's why we're able to go

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:52.590
from early learning through elementary,

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:54.530
middle, high school, core of college

00:18:54.530 --> 00:18:58.603
in math, reading, writing, sciences, etc.

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:03.920
And yeah, it has to have
kind of more people.

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:06.330
It has to because of its nonprofit nature,

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:07.820
it brings more people to the effort.

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:10.130
That's why we're able to
have 40 translated versions

00:19:10.130 --> 00:19:12.120
of Khan Academy, most of which are done

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:14.700
by volunteers around the world.

00:19:14.700 --> 00:19:15.850
So thanks for the question.

00:19:15.850 --> 00:19:18.270
I give the, I could talk more about it

00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:20.210
if folks are interested.

00:19:20.210 --> 00:19:21.043
So let's see.

00:19:21.043 --> 00:19:24.133
Other questions that we're getting.

00:19:27.399 --> 00:19:28.232
Let's see.

00:19:28.232 --> 00:19:31.050
"If I'm using," and this is from Facebook,

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:33.800
Amy asks, "If I'm using Khan Academy

00:19:35.664 --> 00:19:38.417
"for a high school senior
school year, what ground rules

00:19:38.417 --> 00:19:40.240
"do I want to set?"

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:41.870
Meaghan, you wanna take a stab at that?

00:19:41.870 --> 00:19:43.460
I could, I might have some ideas,

00:19:43.460 --> 00:19:46.230
although I haven't raised
high school seniors just yet.

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:49.730
- (laughs) We had a couple of ideas

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:52.730
from the webinar the other
day that I think were,

00:19:52.730 --> 00:19:55.280
you know, maybe something
teachers are comfortable with

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:57.480
but might not put into this framing

00:19:57.480 --> 00:19:58.930
of remote learning.

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:00.840
Usually, at the beginning
of the year a teacher

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:03.200
will set classroom norms
for their students,

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:05.220
and what we find to be most effective

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:08.040
is including your students
in that conversation

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:09.810
about classroom norms.

00:20:09.810 --> 00:20:12.050
You know, things like raising your hand,

00:20:12.050 --> 00:20:15.940
or a process for when you're
gonna using Chromebooks

00:20:15.940 --> 00:20:17.240
in the classroom.

00:20:17.240 --> 00:20:20.480
The same type of things
apply to remote learning.

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:23.950
You have to reestablish
norms with your students

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:26.057
about what your expectations are

00:20:26.057 --> 00:20:28.900
and what behaviors are,
you know, appropriate

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:31.820
for when you're having a
remote learning session.

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:33.900
And so if you're thinking about seniors,

00:20:33.900 --> 00:20:36.620
and I taught high school
science so I can relate

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:39.010
to your seniors for sure,

00:20:39.010 --> 00:20:40.930
I would think about taking time

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:45.210
to set learning norms and
remote classroom norms

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:46.240
with your seniors.

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:48.650
What are the best
practices for your students

00:20:48.650 --> 00:20:50.720
and for your new classroom setup?

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:53.950
And then, by including your
students in that conversation,

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:55.970
they're more likely to
hold each other accountable

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:57.230
and follow those rules 'cause they felt

00:20:57.230 --> 00:20:58.860
like they were a part of the process.

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:01.620
And so being able to establish those norms

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:03.850
for you and your students
makes everyone feel

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:06.290
a little bit more
comfortable in the space.

00:21:06.290 --> 00:21:09.190
Sal, I'm sure you have something
to add onto that as well.

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:12.100
- No, I think that's exactly right.

00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:13.470
You know, this is a time,

00:21:13.470 --> 00:21:16.420
especially for high school seniors where,

00:21:16.420 --> 00:21:19.270
I wrote about it in "One
World Schoolhouse" that a lot

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:24.000
of what we call teenage angst
I believe actually comes

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:25.830
from, in most of human history,

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:28.100
by the time someone was 13 or 14 years old

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:29.630
they were kind of proto adults,

00:21:29.630 --> 00:21:32.100
and they were given
significant responsibility

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:35.320
in most of human history for other people,

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:37.730
for their clan, for their tribe.

00:21:37.730 --> 00:21:41.420
And in modern society
for 13, 14, 15, you know,

00:21:41.420 --> 00:21:43.680
teenagers, we don't give them a lot

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:45.603
of responsibility beyond themselves.

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:49.410
We treat them oftentimes like children,

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:52.040
and I think especially
for high school seniors,

00:21:52.040 --> 00:21:54.490
as you mentioned, Meaghan, the more agency

00:21:54.490 --> 00:21:57.310
and ownership we can
give, and it's authentic,

00:21:57.310 --> 00:21:59.970
they feel like, oh, they
do have a voice in it,

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:02.070
they're setting ground
rules for themselves.

00:22:02.070 --> 00:22:04.580
I have taught, you know,
we have a little lab school

00:22:04.580 --> 00:22:05.880
downstairs from our offices,

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:08.270
and I've taught high school seniors.

00:22:08.270 --> 00:22:10.487
I've actually found that when you say,

00:22:10.487 --> 00:22:12.767
"Hey, what norms," you
know, if I told 'em,

00:22:12.767 --> 00:22:15.187
"Hey, no one can do this,"
or, "You can't use chat

00:22:15.187 --> 00:22:18.060
"this way," or, "You can't
talk while I'm talking,"

00:22:18.060 --> 00:22:18.893
then they'll rebel.

00:22:18.893 --> 00:22:20.937
But if I say, "What
norms do you wanna have,

00:22:20.937 --> 00:22:23.110
"and how will you enforce it as a group?"

00:22:23.110 --> 00:22:26.330
I've seen kids as young
as 12 or 13 step up

00:22:26.330 --> 00:22:28.060
to that challenge and actually come up

00:22:28.060 --> 00:22:29.920
with better (laughs) and
oftentimes stricter norms

00:22:29.920 --> 00:22:31.290
that they're willing to enforce

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:32.700
than I would've put on them.

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:35.120
I think that's definitely
true of high school seniors,

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:36.960
and especially in this
time that everyone's

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:39.610
in their own walk-in closet, so to speak,

00:22:39.610 --> 00:22:40.670
everyone's socially distanced,

00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:42.470
it's even more important to rely

00:22:42.470 --> 00:22:44.210
on their individual agency.

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:46.910
So that makes a ton of sense
what you said, Meaghan.

00:22:46.910 --> 00:22:47.743
So let's see.

00:22:47.743 --> 00:22:49.287
There's a question from Facebook by Judy.

00:22:49.287 --> 00:22:51.047
"Can students retake quizzes and tests

00:22:51.047 --> 00:22:52.087
"as many times as they want

00:22:52.087 --> 00:22:53.980
"or can I put a limit to it?"

00:22:53.980 --> 00:22:57.880
I'll take a first attempt at it.

00:22:57.880 --> 00:23:00.080
The simple answer is yes,
they can take quizzes

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:02.600
and tests as many times as they want.

00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:05.090
And you know, that's actually
an important principle

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:06.700
that we wanted at Khan Academy.

00:23:06.700 --> 00:23:07.780
It actually would've been a lot easier

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:09.380
for us to just make
quizzes that, you know,

00:23:09.380 --> 00:23:12.460
you take once and you're done,
and that's just your score.

00:23:12.460 --> 00:23:15.210
But we're strong believers
in mastery learning,

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:17.010
that if you get an 80 percent on something

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:19.110
that shouldn't be like,
your permanent state

00:23:19.110 --> 00:23:21.180
for the rest of your life,
that you're a C student

00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:24.070
in factoring quadratics or photosynthesis,

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:25.360
that you should have as many attempts

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:26.560
as you need to take it.

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:27.920
But every time you take
it it's actually going

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:30.150
to be a different quiz.

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:32.260
So we've actually written
many, many, many items,

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:34.530
more than most people probably realize,

00:23:34.530 --> 00:23:36.300
to be able to have that capability.

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:37.757
So we want students to say,

00:23:37.757 --> 00:23:39.337
"Oh, I got a 70 percent that time.

00:23:39.337 --> 00:23:41.077
"Let me take another attempt

00:23:41.077 --> 00:23:42.567
"and see if I can improve my score.

00:23:42.567 --> 00:23:44.587
"Or maybe I can go try to
learn it a little better

00:23:44.587 --> 00:23:46.537
"and then try to improve my score."

00:23:48.490 --> 00:23:50.500
I guess there might be situations,

00:23:50.500 --> 00:23:53.420
although, yeah, in general
we favor kids trying

00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:54.520
to take it as many times as possible.

00:23:54.520 --> 00:23:56.883
Meaghan, any things to add to that?

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:01.710
- Sure, one thing I'll add
that teachers find useful,

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:04.610
while we do, and I would
absolutely agree with Sal,

00:24:04.610 --> 00:24:07.933
that we do encourage students
to try and try again,

00:24:08.911 --> 00:24:10.830
there's many people who will say failure,

00:24:10.830 --> 00:24:12.120
or even if you don't
do well the first time,

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:13.180
does not mean failing.

00:24:13.180 --> 00:24:15.370
It's just an opportunity to try again

00:24:15.370 --> 00:24:17.290
and learn something more.

00:24:17.290 --> 00:24:18.620
When we think about teachers though,

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:21.260
and they wanna see how
students are progressing,

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:24.810
if you choose to make
a quiz an assignment,

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:26.590
you do have the opportunity to look

00:24:26.590 --> 00:24:29.460
at how students answered
questions on their first attempt

00:24:29.460 --> 00:24:30.800
and their last attempt.

00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:33.170
So if you wanna see how
they did do that first time

00:24:33.170 --> 00:24:35.410
out the gate, you can look specifically

00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:37.470
at those answers in our teacher reports

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:40.040
and then look at where they ended up.

00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:42.740
So you can see their progress over time,

00:24:42.740 --> 00:24:45.380
and if you wanted a snapshot
of their first attempt

00:24:45.380 --> 00:24:47.480
you can have access to that data,

00:24:47.480 --> 00:24:49.970
but we do wanna continue
to encourage students

00:24:49.970 --> 00:24:51.400
to learn and grow.

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:55.790
And so, exactly what Sal
said, there is no mechanism

00:24:55.790 --> 00:24:58.670
by which to limit the
attempts that they make,

00:24:58.670 --> 00:25:01.290
but you as a teacher can
still see that information.

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:03.943
So in some cases it's
the best of both worlds.

00:25:05.370 --> 00:25:06.910
- Thanks, Meaghan. That's super helpful.

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:10.010
Let's see. We have more questions here.

00:25:10.010 --> 00:25:12.840
We have a question, this is from YouTube

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:14.807
by Algomarble Racer.

00:25:14.807 --> 00:25:16.757
"Sal, how do you make
money from Khan Academy?

00:25:16.757 --> 00:25:18.290
"It is nonprofit."

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:19.622
That's a good question and one we think

00:25:19.622 --> 00:25:20.610
(laughs) about a lot.

00:25:20.610 --> 00:25:22.040
So the simple answer, and for
those of you who don't know,

00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:25.547
a lot of people say, "Well,
isn't Google nonprofit

00:25:25.547 --> 00:25:27.180
"'cause it's free?"

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:29.250
Free and nonprofit don't
mean the same thing,

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:31.540
although Khan Academy
is both of those things.

00:25:31.540 --> 00:25:33.790
A lot of for profit companies, and Google,

00:25:33.790 --> 00:25:35.040
they've actually been great supporters

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:37.520
of Khan Academy, and actually
even in the early days

00:25:37.520 --> 00:25:39.270
they were one of our first funders,

00:25:40.170 --> 00:25:41.980
they're able to offer their services free,

00:25:41.980 --> 00:25:44.320
but they make money in other ways

00:25:44.320 --> 00:25:47.310
through ads or other types of things.

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:49.530
What makes a nonprofit
or for profit different

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:51.700
is Google is owned by shareholders.

00:25:51.700 --> 00:25:52.910
They have owners of the company.

00:25:52.910 --> 00:25:54.860
If you go and buy some
Google stock you are one

00:25:54.860 --> 00:25:57.760
of those owners of Google.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:59.948
Khan Academy has no ownership.

00:25:59.948 --> 00:26:02.640
I have no ownership in Khan Academy,

00:26:02.640 --> 00:26:04.810
or I have as much ownership
as anyone listening to us.

00:26:04.810 --> 00:26:07.710
We all, it's a public good.

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:11.951
And the way that I and
the whole team here live

00:26:11.951 --> 00:26:15.140
(laughs) is that we do get
salaries from Khan Academy,

00:26:15.140 --> 00:26:18.090
and then that is governed by a board.

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:20.960
No one owns Khan Academy,
there's a board of directors.

00:26:20.960 --> 00:26:22.500
The board is made up of,

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:24.760
some of those folks are representative

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:26.140
of some of the major donors

00:26:26.140 --> 00:26:28.440
to Khan Academy, some
of them are other people

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:32.020
who are experts in education
or in other fields,

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:35.060
and they're the ones that govern.

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:38.260
They can hire or fire
me, they set my salary,

00:26:38.260 --> 00:26:40.150
and similarly, they set the budget

00:26:40.150 --> 00:26:41.270
for the whole organization.

00:26:41.270 --> 00:26:43.230
So we take salaries.

00:26:43.230 --> 00:26:44.810
Now, where does that money come from?

00:26:44.810 --> 00:26:47.270
It comes from philanthropic
donations (laughs),

00:26:47.270 --> 00:26:50.020
and you know, every time
I talk about the budget

00:26:50.020 --> 00:26:51.920
of Khan Academy I get a little bit

00:26:51.920 --> 00:26:54.600
of a cortisol boost,
(chuckles) a little stressed,

00:26:54.600 --> 00:26:57.040
'cause it's a large budget,
but it's small compared

00:26:57.040 --> 00:26:58.540
to the impact that it's having.

00:26:58.540 --> 00:26:59.886
Khan Academy's budget is the budget

00:26:59.886 --> 00:27:01.630
of a large high school.

00:27:01.630 --> 00:27:03.770
It's about 60 million dollars a year,

00:27:03.770 --> 00:27:06.160
but we impact, probably
this year, hundreds

00:27:06.160 --> 00:27:08.040
of millions of students every year.

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:10.040
In past years we've had on the order

00:27:10.040 --> 00:27:13.610
of 15, 16, 17, 18 million
students come every month,

00:27:13.610 --> 00:27:14.530
and that's been growing,

00:27:14.530 --> 00:27:16.910
and over the last couple of
weeks that's been growing

00:27:16.910 --> 00:27:18.120
two X and three X.

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:20.270
And so we do need that support, we do need

00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:24.000
those donations from
corporations, from individuals.

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:26.000
As small as you can see on
the screen is three dollars

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:27.290
makes a difference for us.

00:27:27.290 --> 00:27:29.210
And so if you're in a position to benefit

00:27:29.210 --> 00:27:32.050
from Khan Academy and support it

00:27:32.050 --> 00:27:34.540
so that other people can
benefit from Khan Academy,

00:27:34.540 --> 00:27:35.600
please, please do so.

00:27:35.600 --> 00:27:37.513
We really appreciate that.

00:27:38.810 --> 00:27:40.120
Other questions.

00:27:40.120 --> 00:27:41.860
One from YouTube.

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:44.437
Teacher question, comes
from Katharine Curry.

00:27:44.437 --> 00:27:45.547
"If we wanna supplement learning

00:27:45.547 --> 00:27:46.987
"with Khan Academy with our own materials

00:27:46.987 --> 00:27:48.727
"so students can see some of us,

00:27:48.727 --> 00:27:49.560
"what is the best way

00:27:49.560 --> 00:27:51.620
"to deliver our content electronically?"

00:27:51.620 --> 00:27:52.780
I could throw out a few ideas,

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:55.240
and I'm curious what Meaghan thinks.

00:27:55.240 --> 00:27:56.760
One thing that we've been encouraging

00:27:56.760 --> 00:27:58.240
with the schedules,
especially in this time

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:01.310
of social distancing, is
kids can work asynchronously

00:28:01.310 --> 00:28:02.960
on something like Khan Academy,

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:05.450
and it would be amazing
if teachers can organize

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:10.223
video conferences using
Hangout, using Skype,

00:28:11.106 --> 00:28:11.939
you know,

00:28:13.600 --> 00:28:16.040
Zoom, so that they can answer questions,

00:28:16.040 --> 00:28:17.910
do things analogous to
what we've been doing

00:28:17.910 --> 00:28:20.490
with some of these live
classrooms that we've been doing.

00:28:20.490 --> 00:28:23.370
But obviously, you could
do it with your students.

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:26.750
There's also a world where
you can create materials

00:28:26.750 --> 00:28:29.610
yourself at a whiteboard
or use a digital tablet

00:28:29.610 --> 00:28:32.150
the way I have here at Khan
Academy to create videos.

00:28:32.150 --> 00:28:34.130
I know my children's school is doing that.

00:28:34.130 --> 00:28:39.130
And so there's a lot of
work that my kids are doing,

00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:40.670
you know, like a writing assignment.

00:28:40.670 --> 00:28:42.800
So their teacher is making a video,

00:28:42.800 --> 00:28:45.530
putting it on their queue
using the Learning Management

00:28:45.530 --> 00:28:46.970
Systems, so it could be Google Classroom

00:28:46.970 --> 00:28:48.857
or something else, so
that the students know,

00:28:48.857 --> 00:28:50.787
"Okay, here's my checklist for the day.

00:28:50.787 --> 00:28:53.379
"Oh, I gotta watch a video by Mickey,"

00:28:53.379 --> 00:28:55.800
one of my son's teachers.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:56.633
He watches a video by Mickey,
Mickey says something about,

00:28:56.633 --> 00:29:00.357
"How do you analyze certain forms of text?

00:29:00.357 --> 00:29:02.310
"Here's the assignment," then he does it,

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:04.400
then he submits it on the LMS.

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:06.540
But it would be really
cool, what I've been seeing

00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:07.810
some really incredible teachers doing

00:29:07.810 --> 00:29:10.230
even at my own children's
school is they've been

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:13.000
having touchpoints roughly
at least once a day

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:15.610
with students so that
they can ask questions,

00:29:15.610 --> 00:29:16.870
they can understand
how to navigate things.

00:29:16.870 --> 00:29:19.073
Meaghan, any other ideas there?

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:24.250
- I would huge, hugely, hugely endorse

00:29:24.250 --> 00:29:25.910
everything you just said.

00:29:25.910 --> 00:29:28.050
Because as you mentioned,
right, in this time

00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:30.860
of social distancing having
that social experience

00:29:30.860 --> 00:29:34.290
is so much more vital to
student's lives every day.

00:29:34.290 --> 00:29:35.840
They're used to being around each other

00:29:35.840 --> 00:29:39.490
and leaning on their teacher
to share parts of their day

00:29:39.490 --> 00:29:40.830
or to ask questions.

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:42.060
So I think everything you mentioned

00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:45.030
is really wonderful, and if
teachers have the opportunity

00:29:45.030 --> 00:29:48.190
I do like to recommend that,
along with the live experience,

00:29:48.190 --> 00:29:49.470
make sure that it can be recorded.

00:29:49.470 --> 00:29:51.770
Because we know that students just as much

00:29:51.770 --> 00:29:54.150
as adults are struggling
with schedules right now,

00:29:54.150 --> 00:29:55.690
so maybe they are responsible

00:29:55.690 --> 00:29:57.380
for watching a younger sibling

00:29:57.380 --> 00:30:00.670
or they only have access to
a device later on in the day,

00:30:00.670 --> 00:30:02.360
so that if you are going to connect live,

00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:04.430
try and make a recording
as well so that all

00:30:04.430 --> 00:30:07.240
of your students have
access to that material.

00:30:07.240 --> 00:30:08.830
The other piece I might recommend is

00:30:08.830 --> 00:30:11.080
that if you are making
your own assignments

00:30:11.080 --> 00:30:13.430
as well, if you can do them in something

00:30:13.430 --> 00:30:15.250
like Google Docs or Google Slides,

00:30:15.250 --> 00:30:17.410
and allow students to leave
comments for each other,

00:30:17.410 --> 00:30:19.950
maybe one of those
reflection activities that,

00:30:19.950 --> 00:30:22.790
you know, Sal, you've offered
in our daily schedules.

00:30:22.790 --> 00:30:24.730
So that students can
leave really positive,

00:30:24.730 --> 00:30:26.950
reinforcing comments for each other

00:30:26.950 --> 00:30:29.780
so that they're still connecting beyond

00:30:29.780 --> 00:30:31.063
just that one moment.

00:30:32.510 --> 00:30:33.343
- That's a great idea.

00:30:33.343 --> 00:30:34.270
I didn't even thought about that.

00:30:34.270 --> 00:30:37.110
On our schedules we
suggested maybe journaling,

00:30:37.110 --> 00:30:38.950
but there might be a
world where you do like,

00:30:38.950 --> 00:30:41.170
collective journaling where
everyone has a little section,

00:30:41.170 --> 00:30:43.370
and not only do you write yourself,

00:30:43.370 --> 00:30:45.180
but you can see how everyone else's day is

00:30:45.180 --> 00:30:46.610
and how they can think about
it and how they comment.

00:30:46.610 --> 00:30:48.330
I love that idea, Meaghan.

00:30:48.330 --> 00:30:50.753
So time flies when you're having fun.

00:30:50.753 --> 00:30:53.010
(laughs) There's a lot of
really great questions here

00:30:53.010 --> 00:30:53.920
that I wish I could get to,

00:30:53.920 --> 00:30:56.530
but the good thing is
we're doing this every day.

00:30:56.530 --> 00:30:59.840
So come back tomorrow 12 p.m Pacific Time,

00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:03.520
3 p.m. Eastern, or whatever
time zone you might be in.

00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:04.490
Thanks for joining.

00:31:04.490 --> 00:31:06.280
Thanks, Meaghan, for joining.

00:31:06.280 --> 00:31:08.490
As I say every day, these
livestreams are a way

00:31:08.490 --> 00:31:10.290
for us to stay connected in this time

00:31:10.290 --> 00:31:12.660
of social distancing,
and the silver lining

00:31:12.660 --> 00:31:15.360
through this crisis that
we're all going through

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:18.110
is that we're all going
through this together.

00:31:18.110 --> 00:31:19.720
I get a lotta energy by doing this,

00:31:19.720 --> 00:31:21.110
I'm sure I speak for Meaghan

00:31:21.110 --> 00:31:22.810
and the entire Khan Academy team.

00:31:22.810 --> 00:31:26.750
It's really an honor
for us to be of service

00:31:26.750 --> 00:31:29.860
in this time of need, and we
wanna do everything possible

00:31:29.860 --> 00:31:33.390
for every teacher listening,
every parent listening,

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:36.460
every student listening to
make sure you feel supported

00:31:36.460 --> 00:31:38.210
and you feel connected.

00:31:38.210 --> 00:31:40.373
Thank you so much, and see you tomorrow.

